B.Nm=s(["Two birds, inseparable friends, sit on the same tree.","The first hops from branch to branch, tasting fruit. Some fruit is sweet and it sings. Some is bitter and it cries out. It is busy, hungry, pleased, disappointed \u2014 forever reaching for the next fruit, forever sure that this one will finally satisfy.","The second bird sits higher up. It does not eat. It simply watches, calm and bright.","Now and then the first bird, exhausted by sweet and bitter, glances up and sees its companion \u2014 still, untroubled, shining. In that glance its grief falls away. It realises the watcher was never a stranger. It was its own deepest self all along.",'> "Two birds, companions, cling to the same tree. One eats the sweet fruit; the other looks on without eating." \u2014 Mundaka Upanishad',"The Upanishad is describing you. The eating bird is the part of you that wants, reacts and chases \u2014 the autopilot. The watching bird is the witness, the Sakshi, which has been present in every moment of your life without being harmed by any of them.","You do not have to kill the first bird. You only have to look up."],t.s)
B.aaT=s(["witness","upanishad"],t.s)
B.Wf=new A.d3("two-birds","story","Two Birds on One Tree","One eats the fruit. One watches.","Mundaka Upanishad 3.1.1\u20132","assets/images/bird.jpg",3,B.Nm,"Which bird has been running your day so far? When did you last glance up?",'At your next witness ping, before answering, take one second to "look up" \u2014 notice the one who is noticing.')
B.NP=s(["A man gallops down a village road on a powerful horse. He is moving so fast, so purposefully, that he must be on some urgent business.",'A friend standing by the road shouts, "Where are you going?"','The rider calls back over his shoulder: "I don\'t know! Ask the horse!"',"Thich Nhat Hanh loved this story because it is everyone's story. The horse is habit energy \u2014 the momentum of years of reacting the same way. It carries us into the argument, into the fridge, into the phone, into the same old worry, and we sit on top looking busy and certain.","The point is not to shoot the horse. A strong horse is a gift. The point is to remember that you are the rider, and that riders have reins.","> Stopping is the first act of steering."],t.s)
B.aaw=s(["autopilot","zen"],t.s)
B.W5=new A.d3("ask-the-horse","story","Ask the Horse","Where are you going in such a hurry?","A Zen story","assets/images/horse.jpg",2,B.NP,'What "horse" carried you today? Where did it take you that you did not choose?','When you notice speed \u2014 walking fast, typing fast, eating fast \u2014 ask silently: "Where am I going?"')
B.Ph=s(["A traveller passing a camp sees a huge elephant standing quietly, tied to a small wooden peg by a thin rope. One step and the peg would fly out of the ground. Yet the elephant does not move.",'"Why doesn\'t it just walk away?" he asks the keeper.','"When it was a baby," the keeper says, "we tied it with this same rope. It pulled and pulled, but it was too small to break free. After a while it stopped trying. Now it is grown, and it still believes the rope can hold it."','Much of our autopilot is this rope. "I can\'t speak up." "I always do this when I\'m stressed." "I\'m just an anxious person." Beliefs learned when we were smaller and had fewer choices \u2014 still obeyed, never re-tested.',"Awareness is how an elephant notices the rope. Not by fighting it, but by seeing it clearly: thin, old, and optional."],t.s)
B.aaz=s(["conditioning","beliefs"],t.s)
B.Wk=new A.d3("elephant-rope","story","The Elephant and the Rope","The strongest animal, held by a thin rope.","A traditional parable","assets/images/elephant.jpg",3,B.Ph,'Which "I always\u2026" or "I can\'t\u2026" have you never re-tested as an adult?',"Write one rope in your journal tonight. Next to it, write one tiny way to test it tomorrow.")
B.NN=s(["The musk deer catches a wonderful fragrance on the wind. Enchanted, it runs through the forest searching for its source \u2014 through valleys, across streams, deeper and deeper into the hills. It never finds it, because the scent comes from the musk pod in its own body.","> \u0915\u0938\u094d\u0924\u0942\u0930\u0940 \u0915\u0941\u0902\u0921\u0932 \u092c\u0938\u0947, \u092e\u0943\u0917 \u0922\u0942\u0902\u0922\u0947 \u092c\u0928 \u092e\u093e\u0939\u093f\u0964\n\u0910\u0938\u0947 \u0918\u091f \u0918\u091f \u0930\u093e\u092e \u0939\u0948\u0902, \u0926\u0941\u0928\u093f\u092f\u093e \u0926\u0947\u0916\u0947 \u0928\u093e\u0939\u093f\u0902\u0965",'"The musk is in the deer\'s own navel, yet it searches the forest. So the divine lives in every heart, and the world does not see it." \u2014 Kabir',"So much autopilot is a search for a feeling: the next notification for connection, the next snack for comfort, the next achievement for worth. The search keeps us running.","The witness practice is the moment the deer stops running and breathes in. What we were looking for \u2014 peace, presence, enoughness \u2014 is closest when we stop chasing it."],t.s)
B.aaN=s(["seeking","kabir"],t.s)
B.W_=new A.d3("musk-deer","story","The Musk Deer","Searching the whole forest for a scent it carries.","Kabir","assets/images/deer.jpg",2,B.NN,"What feeling are your habits searching for?",'Next time you reach for something to feel better, pause and ask: "Can I find a little of this feeling right here, first?"')
B.Og=s(["The Buddha asked his students: if a person is struck by an arrow, do they feel pain? Yes. And if they are struck by a second arrow, right after the first, do they feel more pain? Of course.","An untrained person, he said, when touched by a painful feeling, worries, grieves and laments. They feel two pains \u2014 the body's and the mind's. It is as if they were shot by two arrows.","The first arrow is life: a harsh comment, a missed train, a headache, a loss. It cannot always be avoided.",'The second arrow is what the mind does next: "This always happens to me." "What is wrong with me?" "They did it on purpose." Replaying, judging, catastrophising. We fire it ourselves, often many times.',"Notice that in your check-ins. The feeling is the first arrow. The mind activity \u2014 ruminating, self-criticism, arguing \u2014 is often the second.","> You may not be able to stop the first arrow. The second one is optional."],t.s)
B.aaP=s(["suffering","buddhism"],t.s)
B.Wc=new A.d3("second-arrow","story","The Second Arrow","The pain you get, and the pain you add.","The Buddha, Sallatha Sutta (SN 36.6)","assets/images/stones.jpg",3,B.Og,"Today, what was the first arrow \u2014 and how many second arrows followed?",'When something stings, say quietly: "First arrow." Then watch, and decline to fire the second.')
B.Ot=s(["At dusk a man walks home and sees a snake coiled on the path. His heart pounds, his legs freeze, his body floods with fear. Everything in him reacts to the snake.","Someone brings a lamp. It is a rope.","Nothing about the rope changed. Only the seeing changed \u2014 and with it, instantly, the fear, the pounding heart, the frozen legs.",'The Vedanta teachers used this image for the deepest illusion of all. But it works at every scale. The "snake" in a short email from your boss. The "snake" in a friend not replying. The body reacts fully to what the mind projects.',"Awareness is the lamp. It doesn't argue with the fear. It just lets you look again."],t.s)
B.aaI=s(["perception","vedanta"],t.s)
B.W3=new A.d3("rope-snake","story","The Rope and the Snake","Fear made of a misreading.","Advaita Vedanta (rajju-sarpa)","assets/images/moon.jpg",2,B.Ot,'What recent "snake" turned out to be a rope?','When fear rises, ask: "What exactly am I seeing \u2014 and what am I adding?"')
B.Oq=s(['An old farmer\'s only horse ran away. The neighbours came: "What terrible luck!" The farmer said, "Maybe."','A week later the horse returned, leading three wild horses. "What wonderful luck!" said the neighbours. "Maybe," said the farmer.','His son tried to ride one of the wild horses, was thrown, and broke his leg. "Such bad luck," the neighbours said. "Maybe."','The next month soldiers came through the village taking every young man to war. The son, with his broken leg, was left behind. "How lucky!" said the neighbours. The farmer said: "Maybe."',"The judging mind labels everything instantly \u2014 good, bad, disaster, win \u2014 and then reacts to its own label. The farmer simply stays with what happened, and waits to see."],t.s)
B.aaG=s(["judging","acceptance"],t.s)
B.W9=new A.d3("maybe","story","Maybe","The old farmer who would not decide what things meant.","Huainanzi (China, 2nd century BCE)","assets/images/field.jpg",3,B.Oq,'What did you label "bad" this week that might still be unfinished?','Next time your mind declares "This is terrible", add one word: "Maybe."')
B.P5=s(["Two monks walking in the rain came to a muddy crossing where a young woman in a silk kimono stood unable to pass.","The older monk, Tanzan, simply picked her up, carried her across and set her down. Then the monks walked on in silence.",'Hours later, as they reached the temple, the younger monk, Ekido, could hold it in no longer. "Monks are not supposed to go near women! Why did you do that?"','Tanzan said: "I put her down at the crossing. Are you still carrying her?"','Every replay of a past moment is a way of still carrying it. The mind called "replaying" in your check-ins is often this: lugging something across hours or years that was set down long ago.'],t.s)
B.aaR=s(["replaying","letting go"],t.s)
B.Wi=new A.d3("carrying","story","Are You Still Carrying Her?","Two monks, a river, and a long walk.","A Zen story (Tanzan and Ekido)","assets/images/stream.jpg",2,B.P5,"What are you still carrying that happened long ago?",'When you notice replaying, say: "Put her down at the crossing." Then feel your feet on the ground.')
B.MQ=s(["One night a neighbour found Mulla Nasruddin on his hands and knees under a street lamp.",'"What are you looking for, Mulla?" "My key." The neighbour knelt to help. After a long search he asked, "Where exactly did you drop it?"','"Inside my house," said Nasruddin.','"Then why are we looking out here?"','"Because there is more light here."',"We look for peace in the places that are easiest to search \u2014 scrolling, buying, snacking, busyness \u2014 because they are bright and close. The key, Nasruddin reminds us with a grin, is inside, where it is darker and less comfortable to look."],t.s)
B.aax=s(["avoidance","humour"],t.s)
B.Wm=new A.d3("streetlight","story","Looking Under the Lamp","Searching where it is easy instead of where it is.","Mulla Nasruddin","assets/images/night_sky.jpg",2,B.MQ,"Where do you search because it is easy, rather than because the answer is there?",'Spend three minutes with the "Who am I?" practice \u2014 looking inside the house.')
B.OB=s(['A man is rowing his boat across a river in the mist when another boat drifts out of the fog and crashes into his. Furious, he shouts: "Watch where you\'re going! Steer away!"',"Then the mist clears, and he sees the other boat is empty. It came loose from its mooring and drifted with the current.","His anger has nowhere to go. There is no one to blame.","Zhuangzi suggests that much of what hits us is an empty boat: people driven by their own currents \u2014 their stress, their fear, their own autopilot \u2014 not aiming at us at all.","> If you can empty your own boat crossing the river of the world, no one will oppose you."],t.s)
B.aat=s(["anger","taoism"],t.s)
B.W6=new A.d3("empty-boat","story","The Empty Boat","Who are you angry at, really?","Zhuangzi","assets/images/lake_boat.jpg",2,B.OB,"Who bumped into you recently? Was anyone really steering?",'When irritation rises at someone, silently say: "Empty boat." Watch what happens to the heat.')
B.Nw=s(["A king asked his wise men for something that would make him happy when he was sad, and humble when he was happy.",'After long thought, they gave him a plain ring. Engraved inside were four words: "This too shall pass."',"In defeat, he read it and found courage. In triumph, he read it and stayed steady.","Every feeling you name in a check-in is weather. Anxious, joyful, bored, furious \u2014 each one arrives, peaks and changes. The Map shows it: the same hour that was stormy on Tuesday is clear on Thursday.","The witness is the sky, which does not pass."],t.s)
B.aaF=s(["impermanence"],t.s)
B.W7=new A.d3("this-too","story","This Too Shall Pass","A ring for the king's best and worst days.","A Persian folk tale","assets/images/evening_sea.jpg",2,B.Nw,"Which feeling today felt permanent while it was here?",'In your next check-in, after naming the feeling, add silently: "\u2026and this too shall pass."')
B.Ml=s(['A grandfather tells his grandson: "Inside every person, two wolves are fighting. One is anger, envy, fear and greed. The other is kindness, courage, calm and joy."','The boy thinks for a while and asks, "Which wolf wins?"','The grandfather says, "The one you feed."','In practice, "feeding" is attention. What you rehearse in your mind grows; what you notice and do not feed weakens. Every time you catch the autopilot and don\'t act on it, one wolf goes a little hungry.',"One gentle correction to the fable: you don't need to hate the first wolf. Seeing it clearly, with curiosity, is often what calms it."],t.s)
B.aav=s(["attention","habits"],t.s)
B.Wg=new A.d3("two-wolves","story","The Two Wolves","The one that wins is the one you feed.",'A modern fable (popularly but doubtfully called "Cherokee")',"assets/images/forest_light.jpg",2,B.Ml,"Which wolf has been eating well this week?","Log one autopilot catch today where you paused instead of feeding the urge.")
B.P8=s(['A young woman in Hakuin\'s village became pregnant and, frightened, told her furious parents that the monk was the father. They stormed to Hakuin and accused him. He listened and said only, "Is that so?"',"When the baby was born they brought it to him to raise. Hakuin had lost his reputation, but he cared for the child tenderly for a year.","Then the young mother, unable to bear it, confessed: the real father was a young man from the fish market. The parents rushed to Hakuin, apologised deeply, and asked for the child back.",'Hakuin handed the baby over and said: "Is that so?"',"The mind called \"wanting approval\" in your check-ins is the opposite of Hakuin. It lives for the village's verdict. Hakuin's steadiness came from somewhere the village could not reach."],t.s)
B.aau=s(["approval","equanimity"],t.s)
B.W4=new A.d3("is-that-so","story","Is That So?","A monk who did not need the village's opinion.","A Zen story about Hakuin","assets/images/hills.jpg",3,B.P8,"Whose opinion is steering you lately?",'When you catch yourself rehearsing what someone thinks of you, try: "Is that so?" \u2014 and return to what you are doing.')
B.Ny=s(["OwnYourMind is not trying to make you meditate for an hour. It is trying to build one skill: noticing, in the middle of ordinary life, that you are on autopilot \u2014 and then choosing. Here is how to use it over a month.","## Week 1 \u2014 Just answer the knocks",'Leave pings at 8\u201310 a day. When one comes, answer honestly for the moment before you looked, not after. "Autopilot" is not failure; it is the most useful data you can give the Map. Don\'t try to change anything yet.',"## Week 2 \u2014 Name things precisely",'Open "More words" when the obvious feeling doesn\'t fit. "Bad" becomes "disappointed" or "drained" or "resentful". Pick up to three, strongest first. Precision is not pedantry: people who can tell their feelings apart regulate them better.',"## Week 3 \u2014 Catch one loop a day",'Look at the Map\'s "What the witness sees". Find your autopilot hour and your most common trigger. Each day, catch one loop in the act with "Catch autopilot" \u2014 and write honestly what it actually gave you.',"## Week 4 \u2014 Plan the gap",'Each morning, set a Sankalpa with an if\u2013then plan aimed at your autopilot hour. Each night, do the review. Watch the "self-caught" number on the Now screen. When most of your moments are self-caught, lower the pings. The app is working when you need it less.',"> The goal is not a perfect streak. The goal is that one day you knock on your own door."],t.s)
B.aaD=s(["guide"],t.s)
B.W1=new A.d3("start-here","article","Your First 30 Days with OwnYourMind","A practical path from knocks to self-knocking.","","assets/images/sunrise.jpg",5,B.Ny,"Which week are you in right now?","Look at the Inner Map today and read the reflections slowly.")
B.Mt=s(["In 2010 two Harvard psychologists, Matthew Killingsworth and Daniel Gilbert, built an iPhone app that pinged people at random moments and asked three questions: How are you feeling? What are you doing? Are you thinking about something other than what you're doing?","Across about a quarter of a million answers from 2,250 people, the mind was wandering in 46.9% of samples \u2014 and in at least 30% of samples for every activity except one. People were less happy when their minds wandered, even when they wandered to pleasant topics. The timing of the answers suggested that the wandering tended to come first and the dip in mood after.","## Wandering is not the enemy","A wandering mind plans, solves problems and makes connections. The brain network most active during rest \u2014 the default mode network \u2014 is also involved in imagining the future and understanding other people. The problem is not wandering. It is unnoticed wandering: hours lost in replaying and worrying without ever choosing to go there.","## Noticing is the skill","Contemplative traditions and modern attention research agree on one thing: the moment you realise you have wandered is the moment of awareness. That moment can be trained, like any skill, by repetition.","This is why OwnYourMind uses random pings rather than scheduled ones. A predictable reminder becomes part of the autopilot. A random one catches the mind in the act \u2014 exactly the method the researchers used to discover the 46.9% in the first place.",'> Every "where was I just now?" is one repetition of the most important exercise you can do.'],t.s)
B.aaH=s(["science","mind-wandering"],t.s)
B.We=new A.d3("why-mind-wanders","article","Why the Mind Wanders \u2014 and Why It Matters","Half of waking life happens somewhere else.","","assets/images/valley.jpg",5,B.Mt,"Where does your mind go most often when it leaves? Check the Map.","Today, count how many times you notice you had wandered. Each one is a win, not a failure.")
B.Mi=s(["Every autopilot behaviour runs on the same loop. A cue (boredom, a notification, 3 p.m., a certain person) triggers a routine (check the phone, open the fridge, snap back), which delivers a reward (relief, novelty, a sense of control). Repeat it enough and the brain stops asking. The cue fires the routine directly.","## Why willpower keeps losing","Fighting the routine head-on rarely lasts, because the brain still believes the reward is worth it. Psychiatrist and researcher Judson Brewer argues that the most sustainable way to change a habit is to update its reward value \u2014 and that this happens when we pay close, curious attention to what the behaviour actually gives us.","In his work, people asked to eat a cigarette-break or a snack mindfully, noticing the real taste and the real feeling afterwards, often discover the reward is much smaller than the craving promised. The brain updates. The pull weakens \u2014 not because they forced it, but because they saw.","## How to use this",'\u2022 Catch the loop in the act with "Catch autopilot". Name the cue.',"\u2022 Notice the urge in the body. Rate it.",'\u2022 Whether you resist or not, write what it actually gave you \u2014 honestly. "Two minutes of relief, then more restless" is gold.',"\u2022 Look at your loops on the Map. Repeated cues are where an if\u2013then plan pays off most.",'> Curiosity is a better reward than the habit. "What does this actually feel like?" is a question the autopilot can\'t answer for you.'],t.s)
B.aaE=s(["habits","science"],t.s)
B.Wl=new A.d3("habit-loop","article","How Autopilot Is Built","Cue, routine, reward \u2014 and how to update the reward.","","assets/images/road.jpg",6,B.Mi,"What does your most common autopilot actually give you, honestly?","Next time you give in to an urge, do it slowly and with full attention \u2014 and log what it truly felt like.")
B.Ou=s(['In a 2007 brain-imaging study led by Matthew Lieberman at UCLA, people looked at pictures of angry or frightened faces. When they simply chose a word for the emotion \u2014 "angry", "scared" \u2014 the response in the amygdala, an alarm centre of the brain, was lower than when they matched faces by other features. A region of the right prefrontal cortex became more active. Naming a feeling, it seems, starts to regulate it.',"## Precision helps more","Psychologists call the ability to describe feelings precisely emotional granularity, or emotion differentiation. A review by Todd Kashdan, Lisa Feldman Barrett and Patrick McKnight (2015) brought together studies suggesting that people who distinguish their negative feelings finely \u2014 irritated versus resentful versus disappointed \u2014 tend to cope better, and are less likely to fall back on blunt tools like drinking or lashing out.",'That is why OwnYourMind now offers more than eighty feelings in ten families, an intensity scale, and room for your own word. "Bad" tells your brain almost nothing about what to do. "Disappointed and tired" points toward rest and a conversation. "Anxious and excited" might be readiness, not danger.',"## How to get more precise","\u2022 Start with the family: afraid, angry, sad, ashamed, unsettled, low, peaceful, joyful, loving, engaged.","\u2022 Then ask: which word in this family fits exactly?","\u2022 Allow blends. Most real moments hold two or three feelings at once.",'\u2022 Rate the strength. "Faintly irritated" and "furious" call for different responses.',"> The more exactly you name it, the less it names you."],t.s)
B.aaC=s(["feelings","science"],t.s)
B.W8=new A.d3("name-it","article","Name It to Tame It","Why the exact word for a feeling matters.","","assets/images/flowers.jpg",5,B.Ou,"Which feeling word did you use today that could be more exact?",'In your next check-in, open "More words" and find the precise one \u2014 even if it takes ten seconds longer.')
B.OR=s(["Notice how much easier it is to give a friend good advice than to give it to yourself. Psychologist Ethan Kross and colleagues wondered whether a small change in language could borrow that distance.",'In a series of six studies published in 2014, people about to face stress \u2014 like giving a speech with little preparation \u2014 were asked to reflect on their feelings either using "I" or using their own name and "you". Those using their name and "you" reported less distress, were rated as performing better, and spent less time ruminating afterwards. They tended to see the task as a challenge rather than a threat.',"## Why OwnYourMind calls you by name",'The Inner Voice always speaks to you as "you", and uses your name. "Pallav, you caught the autopilot" lands differently from "I caught the autopilot". It is the witness addressing the person \u2014 calm, slightly outside the storm.',"## Try it yourself",'\u2022 When upset, silently ask: "What is going on for you right now, [name]?"','\u2022 When deciding: "What would you advise a friend in your place?"',"\u2022 When self-critical: notice the tone. Would you speak this way to someone you love?","> Distance is not coldness. It is the space where wisdom has room to speak."],t.s)
B.aaM=s(["self-talk","science"],t.s)
B.Wa=new A.d3("talk-like-friend","article","Talk to Yourself Like a Friend",'The science of saying "you" instead of "I".',"","assets/images/stillness.jpg",4,B.OR,'How does your inner critic speak \u2014 in "I" or in "you"? Is it kind?',"Next time you are stressed, talk yourself through it using your own name, silently.")
B.P6=s(['Goals say what you want. Implementation intentions say when, where and how you will act: "If I reach for my phone without deciding to, then I will take one breath and ask who is reaching."',"Peter Gollwitzer and Paschal Sheeran's 2006 review of 94 tests found a medium-to-large average effect of these plans on reaching goals. A much larger 2024 update with 642 tests, after correcting for publication bias, found the effect is smaller than first thought \u2014 but still positive. In plain words: they help, they are not magic.","## Why they work",'An if\u2013then plan hands the decision to the cue in advance. When the "if" shows up, the "then" is already loaded, so you don\'t have to summon willpower in the moment \u2014 the very moment when the autopilot is strongest.',"## Writing good ones",'\u2022 Make the "if" a specific, noticeable moment \u2014 a place, a time, a feeling, an action.','\u2022 Make the "then" tiny and immediate: one breath, feel your feet, wait 90 seconds.','\u2022 Aim it at your real weak spot. The Map\'s autopilot hour and top trigger are perfect "ifs".',"> Decide once, in the calm of the morning, so you don't have to decide in the storm."],t.s)
B.aaK=s(["planning","science"],t.s)
B.Wd=new A.d3("if-then","article","If\u2013Then: The Smallest Plan That Works","Implementation intentions, honestly.","","assets/images/bridge.jpg",4,B.P6,"What is the one moment of the day where an if\u2013then plan would change the most?","Set tomorrow's Sankalpa with an if\u2013then aimed at your autopilot hour.")
B.O3=s(["The psychologist G. Alan Marlatt, a pioneer of relapse prevention, taught a simple image: an urge is like a wave. It builds, crests and subsides. Most of us never find this out, because we act on the urge as soon as it starts rising \u2014 and acting teaches the brain that only acting makes it stop.","## Surfing instead of fighting","Urge surfing means staying with the urge as a set of body sensations \u2014 heat in the chest, restlessness in the hands, a pull in the belly \u2014 and watching them change. You don't fight, and you don't obey. You ride.","People who try this are often surprised by how quickly the peak passes when it isn't fed, and how each wave they ride without acting tends to make the next one a little smaller.","## In the app","The Urge surf practice asks you to rate the urge every 30 seconds and draws your own wave as you go. Watching that line fall is a powerful lesson the body remembers.","> You are not the wave. You are the one standing on the board."],t.s)
B.aaQ=s(["urges","practice"],t.s)
B.VZ=new A.d3("urges-waves","article","Urges Are Waves","They rise, peak and fall \u2014 if you don't feed them.","","assets/images/waves.jpg",4,B.O3,"What urge do you usually obey before it has a chance to peak?","Next time that urge arrives, open Urge surf and ride one wave to the end.")
B.Pp=s(["It is ironic that an awareness app lives on a phone. But the phone is where much of our autopilot lives, so it is also the best place to catch it.","## Notice the reach",'Phone checking is often triggered by tiny discomforts: a pause in conversation, waiting, a hard task, a flicker of boredom or loneliness. The reach happens before the thought "I want to check".',"## Small changes that help",'\u2022 One breath before unlocking. Ask: "What am I looking for?"',"\u2022 Turn off non-human notifications. Let people reach you, not apps.","\u2022 Move the most pulling apps off the home screen, or into a folder on the last page.","\u2022 Try greyscale for a day. Colour is part of the reward.","\u2022 Keep the phone out of the bedroom. The first and last minutes of the day set its tone.","\u2022 Use the always-on anchor: each time you see it in the status bar, it is a tiny knock.","## Catch, don't punish",'When you notice you\'ve been scrolling for twenty minutes, that noticing is the practice. Log it with "Catch autopilot", note the cue, and write what it actually gave you. Shame keeps the loop going; curiosity loosens it.'],t.s)
B.aaJ=s(["phone","habits"],t.s)
B.W0=new A.d3("phone","article","The Phone in Your Hand","Using a phone to break phone autopilot.","","assets/images/glass.jpg",5,B.Pp,"What feeling usually comes just before you reach for your phone?",'Today, take one breath before every unlock and silently ask "What am I looking for?"')
B.P9=s(["Before you know you're angry, the jaw has clenched. Before you know you're anxious, the breath has gone shallow and the shoulders have risen. The ability to sense these internal signals is called interoception, and it is a big part of how emotions are built and recognised.","## Why the body step matters",'The check-in ends with the body because the body is often the most honest witness. Thoughts can rationalise; a knot in the stomach does not. Over weeks, you may notice your personal signatures: "tight shoulders" before snapping, "racing heart" before avoiding, "heavy" before scrolling.',"The body list in the app is now organised by region \u2014 head and face, throat and chest, belly, shoulders and back, arms and legs \u2014 so you can be specific.","## A one-minute body check","\u2022 Unclench the jaw. Drop the tongue from the roof of the mouth.","\u2022 Lower the shoulders an inch.","\u2022 Feel the belly move with the breath.","\u2022 Feel the weight of your body on the chair or the floor.","> The body is always in the present. That is why it is the door back."],t.s)
B.aay=s(["body","science"],t.s)
B.W2=new A.d3("body-first","article","Your Body Knows First","Interoception: the early-warning system.","","assets/images/body.jpg",4,B.P9,"What is your body's early sign of your most common reaction?","Do the one-minute body check before you open your phone next.")
B.Mh=s(['A common trap in awareness practice: the "watcher" becomes a new inner critic. "There you go again, on autopilot. Pathetic." That isn\'t the witness. That is the judging mind wearing a meditation shawl.',"## What the witness is like",'In the Upanishads, the Sakshi is pure seeing \u2014 present to everything, disturbed by nothing. Buddhist teachers describe mindfulness as knowing what is happening while it is happening, with friendliness. Internal Family Systems therapy describes a "Self" that is curious, calm and compassionate toward every part of us, including the difficult ones.',"None of these descriptions include contempt.","## How to tell the difference",'\u2022 The critic says "You shouldn\'t\u2026". The witness says "Ah, this is happening."',"\u2022 The critic tightens the body. The witness softens it.","\u2022 The critic wants you to feel bad so you change. The witness simply sees, and change follows the seeing.",'If you notice the critic, that\'s fine \u2014 note it as "self-criticism" in your check-in. The one who noticed the critic was the witness.'],t.s)
B.aaS=s(["witness","philosophy"],t.s)
B.Wb=new A.d3("witness-not-critic","article","The Witness Is Not Another Critic","How to watch the mind without judging it.","","assets/images/witness.jpg",4,B.Mh,"When you catch yourself on autopilot, what is the tone of your inner voice?",'Next time you notice a slip, say only: "Ah. This is happening." Nothing more.')
B.OQ=s(['The Roman philosopher Seneca described a nightly habit: when the lamp was put out and the house fell quiet, he went back over his whole day, "hiding nothing from myself, passing nothing by." He did it not to punish himself but to learn, like a teacher marking work kindly.',"Ignatius of Loyola built a similar practice, the Examen. Many traditions have something like it, because it works in a practical way: reviewing the day turns scattered moments into patterns you can remember.","## What to look for","\u2022 When was I awake today \u2014 fully here?","\u2022 When was I asleep at the wheel?","\u2022 What would the witness do differently tomorrow \u2014 one small, specific thing?","\u2022 One thing I'm grateful for that I would usually miss.","## The loop with the morning",'The evening review feeds the morning Sankalpa. "Mid-afternoon scrolling" at night becomes "If it is 3 p.m. and I reach for my phone, then I will stand up and stretch" in the morning. Over weeks, the day you review at night is the day you notice during the next one.'],t.s)
B.aaL=s(["review","stoicism"],t.s)
B.Wh=new A.d3("evening-review","article","Why the Evening Review Works","What Seneca knew about memory and change.","","assets/images/evening_sea.jpg",4,B.OQ,"What would tonight's review reveal about today?","Do tonight's evening review in the Journal tab \u2014 it takes three minutes.")
B.NH=s(["If you look at your Map and see autopilot clustering in the late afternoon or late at night, you're not alone, and you're not weak. When we are tired, stressed or hungry, the brain leans harder on habit. The slower, deliberate kind of thinking takes effort, and effort is the first thing a tired system cuts.","Research on sleep loss consistently finds that it impairs attention and self-control, and stress tends to shift behaviour from deliberate goals toward ingrained habits. The details are still debated, but the practical lesson is clear.","## Practical lessons",'\u2022 Protect sleep before blaming willpower. Many "discipline" problems are sleep problems.',"\u2022 Put your hardest if\u2013then plan at your most tired hour, because that is when the autopilot is strongest.","\u2022 Eat and drink water before your known autopilot window.","\u2022 At night, keep the phone out of reach. The tired brain will not argue with a phone that is in the other room.","> Kindness to the body is not a detour from awareness. It is how awareness gets the energy to stay awake."],t.s)
B.aaO=s(["sleep","stress"],t.s)
B.Wj=new A.d3("tired-autopilot","article","Tired Brains Choose Habit","Sleep, stress and the strength of autopilot.","","assets/images/moon.jpg",4,B.NH,"Does your autopilot hour line up with when you are most tired or hungry?","Tonight, put your phone to charge outside the bedroom.")
